that direction proper for any \code{p}, which is what made it the default: the design of
Odegard et al. (2010) has 80 fixed classes, which no \code{df} covers. Measured in
\code{validation/gibbs_probit_odegard.R} (2000 animals with one binary record each, 80 fixed
classes, \code{h2 = 0.2} on the liability; 10 data sets, 4 chains of 25000 from dispersed
starts): with flat fixed effects every prior, \code{half_t(df = 4)} included, took the
median of \code{var(animal)} to 1210 to 1760 in 8 of 10 data sets against 0.25 planted
(drift between the chain halves +830 to +915, SE about 150). With \code{prior_fixed = 1} the
drift fell to +21 (SE 8) under \code{half_t()}, +11 (SE 4) under \code{half_t(df = 4)} and +2 (SE
5) under the default, \code{half_t(df = 4)} with \code{px}. Under the default the median sat at
0.24 to 1.42 in 6 of the 10 data sets and at 15 to 62 in the other 4, and the 95\% interval
covered the planted 0.25 in 3 of 10 (R-hat up to 1.75); without \code{px}, 0.24 to 1.14 in 7
and 11 to 49 in 3, coverage 3 of 10 (R-hat up to 1.79); under \code{half_t()}, 4 of 10 sat at
16 to 70. 25000 iterations do not cover that posterior: with and without \code{px} the target
is the same, yet 3 of the 10 data sets sat above 10 under one arm and not the other. Its
standard deviation matters: with \code{prior_fixed = 3} (without \code{px}) the median went back
to 262 to 408 in 8 of 10 data sets (drift +111, SE 23). Why 4 data sets still drift
under the default is unresolved: the full sibs of those data agree in category as at
\code{h2 = 0.2} on the liability (0.517 to 0.553 of the pairs, against 0.532 expected there
and 0.667 at \code{h2 = 1}), but the two with the highest agreement (0.553 and 0.549) sat high
with and without \code{px} and the two with the lowest (0.517) under neither, which fits slow
mixing of the data augmentation as well as a tail of the posterior.
With many small fixed classes the variance components move with this prior. In
\code{validation/gibbs_probit_default.R} (modes classes and incidencia, run without \code{px}, which
then was not part of the default; mode classes: a sire model with 100 sires and 120
to 148 contemporary groups of 10 records, those with a single category dropped, 1100 to
1670 records, \code{var(sire)} 0.1 planted on the liability, 30 data sets per design) every
choice put the posterior mean of \code{var(sire)} above 0.1, and the looser the prior the
further: 0.169, 0.214 and 0.225 (SE about 0.014) under \code{prior_fixed = 1}, 3 and \code{Inf},
the 95\% interval covering 0.1 in 0.70, 0.47 and 0.40 of the data sets, with group
effects of standard deviation 0.7 on the liability; 0.132, 0.198 and 0.216, coverage
0.87, 0.73 and 0.60, with 1.5. Reversing the row order, which changes the reference
class, moved the mean by -0.0005 (SE 0.0005). Read the intercept with the prior in mind:
at an incidence of 2\% the liability of the reference cell is about -2, two prior
standard deviations from 0, so a reference cell with few records is pulled toward an
incidence of one half. With large classes it was not (mode incidencia: 3000 records in
three herd-year classes, 100 sires): at 2\% the intercept sat -0.006 (SE 0.017) from its
true value on the liability under \code{prior_fixed = 1} and -0.043 (0.018) with flat fixed
effects, and \code{var(sire)} was 0.108 and 0.118 against 0.1 (coverage 0.97 in both; median
bulk ESS about 150 from 2 chains of 20000); at 10\%, 0.104 under every choice.}

\item{px}{\code{TRUE} adds the Haar PX-DA move (Wang and Roy 2018, Algorithm 2; Hobert and
Marchev 2008) to the probit chain: after the liabilities \code{l} are drawn, every one is
multiplied by \code{g}, with \code{g^2 ~ Gamma(n/2, rate = q/2)} and \verb{q = l'l - l'W M^-1 W'l}
(\code{M} the coefficient matrix of the mixed-model equations) the quadratic form of \code{l}
with the locations integrated out, and the locations are drawn from the rescaled
liabilities. The sign of each liability, so the binary record, does not change under
a positive factor, and the move leaves the posterior unchanged; it costs one
chi-square draw and two dot products per iteration. \code{family = "probit"} only. Chains
with and without it agree within Monte Carlo error on a model with two variance
components (16 chains of 3000 each way, 20 data sets: largest |z| 2.70 on the
components, 3.02 on the fixed effects, 3.44 over 70 levels;
\code{validation/gibbs_probit_px.R}), and both reproduce a fixed-effect posterior known
by quadrature. A decision, and not the rule written before the runs, puts it on by
default. That rule keeps a sampler step only if the lower 95\% bound of its paired ESS
ratio passes 1. The move leaves the posterior unchanged. Hobert and
Marchev (2008) prove Haar PX-DA at least as efficient as plain data augmentation for a
two-block chain; here that covers the step between the liabilities and the locations at
fixed variance components, not the whole sweep, which draws the components, the
auxiliaries of the half-t and the ASIS step in between, so it is no guarantee for this
chain. \code{px = FALSE} is the choice the rule makes. \code{NULL} (the default) is \code{TRUE} with
\code{family = "probit"}, \code{prior} left unset and at least one component estimated, and \code{FALSE}
otherwise: with a prior named, with \code{theta_fixed}, and with \code{family = "gaussian"}, where
\code{TRUE} is refused}

\item{start}{where the chains start: \code{NULL} (the default) for the automatic start, a
numeric vector of the components in the order of the columns of \code{samples} (the
residual included; with \code{family = "probit"} its entry is ignored, the residual being
1), or a list with one such vector per chain, for the dispersed starts that the
between-chain R-hat needs. A named vector must carry the names of \code{samples} in their
order. Every estimated covariance group must start positive-definite. Not with
\code{theta_fixed}}

\item{allow_improper}{\code{TRUE} runs \code{prior = "jeffreys"}, \code{"flat"} or \code{"uniform_sd"} with
\code{family = "probit"} and an estimated random term, which is otherwise refused: under
\code{"jeffreys"} the posterior is improper, and under the other two it is where the random
effects can reproduce the category of every record, as with one record per animal (see
\code{prior}). It changes nothing else}

\item{asis}{\code{TRUE} adds, after the draw of each covariance group (a scalar term being a
group of one), the ancillarity-sufficiency interweaving step of Yu and Meng (2011), in
the form Kastner and Fruhwirth-Schnatter (2014) give it: with \code{L = chol(C)} the
effects are rewritten as \verb{U~ = U L^-T}, whose prior does not depend on \code{C}, and \code{C} is
drawn again given \verb{U~} and the rest of the chain. The part of the group in the linear
predictor is linear in the lower triangle of \code{L}, so the proposal is the Gaussian
posterior of that triangle in the regression of the partial residual on the
\code{d(d+1)/2} columns \verb{Z_j U~_m} (\code{m <= j}), with noise \code{var(residual)} (1 on the
liability with \code{family = "probit"}), and the Metropolis-Hastings acceptance is the
ratio of the prior density of \verb{L L'} (given \code{a} under a half-t prior) times the
Jacobian \verb{2^d prod_i L_ii^(d - i + 1)}; a proposal with a diagonal entry of \code{L} at or
below zero is rejected. On acceptance \code{U} becomes \verb{U~ L*'}. The target of the step is
the conditional of \code{L} given \verb{U~}, which has to be integrable near a zero diagonal entry
of \code{L}, where the likelihood stays positive. It is under \code{"flat"}, \code{"uniform_sd"},
\code{c(df = , scale = )} and \code{"half_t"} (the last two through the factor
\verb{exp(-s0 / (2 L_dd^2))}), and not under \code{"jeffreys"}, whose prior times the Jacobian
grows like \verb{prod_i L_ii^-i} there: \code{asis = TRUE} is refused on any estimated group or
scalar term under \code{"jeffreys"}, which only a prior named puts there. Run anyway on the
direct-maternal design of \code{validation/gibbs_asis.R} (mode jeffreys: 960 records,
\code{"jeffreys"} on the group and on a scalar term, 16 chains of 3000, 5 data sets) the step
held 12 to 16 of the 16 chains at a singular correlation in every data set, against none
without it, and accepted 0.7\% to 1.0\% of its proposals on the group; under \code{"flat"} on
the same data no chain was held, with or without it (acceptance 0.79 to 0.86). It costs
no new factorization, and the fit reports the share of
proposals accepted after the burn-in in \code{asis_acceptance} (per group, pooled over the
chains; \code{chain_asis_acceptance} per chain). Not with \code{theta_fixed}. Chains with and without it
agree within Monte Carlo error on every component, fixed effect and level
(\code{validation/gibbs_asis.R}: a 2 x 2 direct-maternal group plus a scalar term under
\code{c(df = 4, scale = 0.3)}, under \code{"half_t"} and under the Gaussian default (\code{"flat"} on
the group, the half-t on the scalar term), and a 3 x 3 random regression, on about 270
records, and a probit random regression of the sire plus a scalar term on 600 binary
records; 16 chains each way, 20 data sets), while on
the 2 x 2 groups the same gate rejects the step without the Jacobian and without the
Metropolis-Hastings correction. The chain is the same with 1 and 4 threads, and under
\code{"half_t"} it stays equivariant to the unit of the trait and the scale of a covariate.
The acceptance was 0.20 to 0.88 on the small data of the gate. It was 0.007 to 0.042
on its 3 x 3 random regression, where the step does little. \code{NULL} (the default)
runs the step, when \code{prior} is left unset, on every estimated term of a Gaussian trait,
covariance groups and scalar terms, and on every estimated covariance group of a probit,
whose scalar terms are left out because the step was not measured there (their rows of
\code{asis} show no attempt). With a prior named, with \code{theta_fixed}, or with nothing for the
step to act on, \code{NULL} is \code{FALSE}; \code{TRUE} runs it on every group and scalar term. Against
the same chains with \code{asis = FALSE}, from the same seeds (\code{"flat"} on the group, the
half-t on the scalar terms, both arms with \code{"uniform_sd"} on the residual): on the
direct-maternal groups at -0.3 and -0.8 the smallest bulk ESS rose 5.50 (4.77 to 6.35)
and 5.18 (4.31 to 6.22) times per iteration, and on \code{rn()} with \code{d = 3} and 2 10.63 (9.02 to 12.52) and 7.36 (6.89 to
7.86) times (\code{validation/gibbs_group_prior.R}, mode padrao, 30 data sets each), where
without it the bulk ESS of 400 and the R-hat under 1.01 came together in 0, 0, 0 and 18
of 30 at the same length; the posterior means stayed within 0.033 posterior SD of those
without it, and on 200 records the step gave no clear gain (1.14, 0.65 to 2.00, at 2
chains of 10000). On a scalar term alone (\code{validation/gibbs_scalar_prior.R}, mode asis:
\code{random()} or \code{animal()} with 5, 20 or 200 levels on 2000 records, planted 0 or 0.05 of
\code{var(y)}, 30 data sets per case, 2 chains of 50000) it rose 2.12 times per iteration
(1.90 to 2.36) at 0.05 and 3.47 times (2.97 to 4.04) at 0, from 1.09 to 13.6 times
across the twelve cases, which passed the rule written before the run (the lower bound
above 1 at 0.05, no case at 0 with its upper bound below 1); with 200 levels and nothing
planted the data sets with a bulk ESS of 400 and an R-hat under 1.01 went from 0 and 1
of 30 to 29 and 30 (\code{random()} and \code{animal()}), the chains held at zero fell from 8 to
3 of 360, coverage of 0.05 stayed at 171 of 180, and the posterior
means agreed within Monte Carlo error. In that one-term model the step costs as much as
the rest of an iteration (1.36 to 1.96 times the CPU), so per second it lost with 5
levels (0.82 and 0.93 times at a planted 0, 0.77 and 0.69 at 0.05) and on \code{random()}
with 20 levels at 0.05 (0.94, 0.91 to 0.97). In \code{random(cg) + animal() + maternal()} on
2400 records (mode misto, the default with \code{"uniform_sd"} on the residual) the step on
the group and on \code{cg} together cost 1.02 and 1.10 times the CPU, in two runs of 3
interleaved repetitions on a machine shared with other jobs}
}
\value{
samples matrix (kept iterations x parameters), posterior \code{mean} and \code{sd},
the posterior mean/sd of every random effect (\code{ebv}, \code{ebv_sd}) and of every fixed
effect (\code{b}, \code{b_sd}, named \code{term=level} as in \code{\link[=model]{model()}}, whose parametrization note
applies: dropped columns are in \code{dropped_x} and only contrasts compare against a
reference-level convention), \code{dense_block} as in \code{\link[=model]{model()}}, \code{start},
the components the chain starts from (the first chain's, with several), and the
convergence diagnostics: \code{diagnostics} is \code{\link[=mcmc_diagnostics]{mcmc_diagnostics()}} of the fit, and \code{ess}
(the multi-chain bulk-ESS), \code{rhat} (rank-normalized split R-hat, also for one chain,
from its two halves) and \code{geweke} (the Geweke z combined over the chains, with the
spectral density at zero, on the scale given by the \code{start_scale} column of the
diagnostics) are its columns for the components; they are \code{NA} and the \code{message} says
why if the diagnostics fail. \code{n_iter}, \code{burnin}, \code{thin} and \code{n_expected} (the draws
each chain keeps when it runs to the end) record the run, and \code{fixed_components} marks
the components the model holds (\code{theta_fixed}, the probit residual), which the diagnostics report as \code{"fixed"}. \code{residual} is the posterior mean
of the realized residual, one value per row of \code{data} (\code{NA} where the record did not
enter), which by linearity is \verb{y - X E[b] - Z E[u]} (the liability for the probit),
\code{fitted} and \code{fitted_fixed} the posterior means \verb{X E[b] + Z E[u]} and \verb{X E[b]} (on
the liability scale for the probit), and \code{pdm_residuals} the stored draws described
in \code{residual_draws}; with several chains all four pool the chains pooled for \code{b} and
\code{ebv}. \code{\link[=residuals.breeding_fit]{residuals.breeding_fit()}} reads them, with \code{missing_code} as given.
\code{b_standardized} (internal) is \code{E[b]} on the engine's standardized scale (each
covariate centred and scaled), pooled the same way. \code{prior_scale} is
the scale \code{A} of every variance under a half-t prior, named as in \code{samples} (empty
under the other priors and with \code{theta_fixed}). \code{prior}, \code{prior_scalar},
\code{prior_residual} and \code{prior_fixed} are the priors the chain used: that of the
covariance groups, that of the scalar terms, that of the residual (\code{NULL} with
\code{family = "probit"}) and the standard deviation of the normal prior of the fixed
effects (\code{Inf} for flat); \code{prior_fallback} names the covariance groups that the
default moved from \code{"flat"} to \code{\link[=half_t]{half_t()}} for having too few levels (empty when none),
and \code{px} is the PX-DA move as the chain ran it (\code{TRUE} or \code{FALSE}). When the ASIS step
ran, \code{asis} holds its counts per group after the burn-in (\code{attempts}, \code{accepted}, and
\code{outside} for proposals with a diagonal entry of \code{L} at or below zero), summed over the
chains pooled, \code{asis_acceptance} the share accepted per group (\code{NA} for a group the step
leaves out: the scalar terms under the default of a probit) and \code{chain_asis_acceptance} that share per chain (groups x chains, one column
with one chain); \code{asis} is \code{NULL} when the step did not run
}
\description{
The structure checks of \code{\link[=model]{model()}} (see its section "Structure checks") apply here: a
design that one of them finds unidentified stops the chain unless \verb{theta_fixed =} gives
the components (a random term inside a fixed effect leaves the chain walking the prior
of that variance), and the warnings go to \code{message}. Those checks read the structure of
the design; a ridge that only the likelihood shows is not among them, and the chain has
no SINGULAR verdict where \code{\link[=model]{model()}} reports the information matrix SINGULAR. On 30
simulated data sets of 1600 records on unrelated founders only (\code{var(animal)} against
\code{var(residual)}, \code{prior = "jeffreys"}), \code{\link[=model]{model()}} was SINGULAR in 30 of 30. The
message of the chain was empty in 30 of 30. On such a ridge the chain wanders (R-hat up to 2.5 over
the separate components, while the combinations the data identify are recovered), and
under \code{prior = "jeffreys"} it can end on that boundary. Fit \code{\link[=model]{model()}} with the same
formula first and read its SINGULAR verdict. The h2 row of \code{summary()} and of
\code{\link[=mcmc_diagnostics]{mcmc_diagnostics()}} follows the rule of \code{\link[=h2]{h2()}}: the direct genetic term,
\verb{4 var(sire)} in a sire model, the components as estimated (the base of Gamma with
metafounders) and the grandsire part of \code{sire(mgs =)} in the denominator; with only \code{pe()}
or \code{random()} terms there is no h2 row.
}
